  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThrough Tonight: The oppressive heat and humidity will continue \ntoday but a weak backdoor cold front may help limit the heat in the \nnorth by late afternoon. Early morning satellite imagery/RAP \nanalysis still showed the circulation around a large 594dam high \ncentered over the southern Plains but also a closed low centered \nover the New England states. This closed low was helping support a \nweak cold front dropping into the Tennessee valley that will try and \ndrop into the northern portions of our CWA later this afternoon. Our \nCWA will remain on the eastern periphery of the large high through \nthe forecast period while maintaining northwest flow aloft across our \nCWA that will help the weak cold front drift south across our CWA \ntonight. It appears that the heat and humidity will again result in \npeak heat index values above 110F across our south and between the \nMississippi river and Interstate 55 while our western and northeast \nzones see peak heat index values between 106F-110F before any relief \nfrom convection associated with the approaching cold front. An \n\"Extreme Heat Warning\" and \"Heat Advisory\" remains in effect today \nfor the respective areas. The chance for rain this afternoon will be \nconfined to our northern zones through early evening before rain \nchances spread south to the Highway 84 corridor tonight. /22/\n\nThursday Onwards...\n\nThe long-term forecast remains on track: A front is expected to sag \ninto the area and stall out through the area for the rest of the \nweek. This front should allow for slightly drier and cooler \nconditions for the northern portions of the CWA during the latter \nhalf of the week. Increased chances for convective activity and \ncloud coverage will also take hold due to the surface confluence \nfrom the aforementioned front which could provide a few degree dip; \nmitigating heat threat concerns. With upper flow becoming more \nnortherly and northwesterly during this time frame, we will see \npotential for one or two convective complexes moving into the area \nor developing over the area.\n\nBy the end of the work week, an upper shortwave looks to become \norganized enough that a more typical front will develop across the \nCentral Plains and push into the area around the late \nFriday/Saturday time frame. This will result in continued higher \nrain chances across the area heading into the weekend. This system \nwill also lose momentum and stall over the area which will bring \nhigher than average diurnal rain chances across the area to finish \nout the period./OAJ/",
